1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,580
Picture switching on a Windows PC
and finding no taskbar,

2
00:00:03,660 --> 00:00:07,260
no Start button,
and not a single icon on the desktop.

3
00:00:07,817 --> 00:00:10,017
Instead there's a thin bar along the top,

4
00:00:10,097 --> 00:00:15,577
a menu that pops up wherever you click,
and four virtual desktops side by side.

5
00:00:16,084 --> 00:00:17,344
It looks like Linux.

6
00:00:17,564 --> 00:00:22,364
But your programs, your drivers and your
files are exactly where you left them.

7
00:00:22,764 --> 00:00:24,064
It's still Windows.

8
00:00:24,888 --> 00:00:26,908
Only the desktop has changed.

9
00:00:27,188 --> 00:00:30,448
And the switch that changes it
is a single setting.

10
00:00:31,130 --> 00:00:34,450
Windows keeps it in the registry,
in a value called Shell.

11
00:00:35,270 --> 00:00:37,810
On almost every Windows PC,
it says explorer.exe.

12
00:00:39,932 --> 00:00:42,032
Write the name
of a different program there,

13
00:00:42,112 --> 00:00:46,252
sign out and sign back in,
and Windows starts that program instead.

14
00:00:47,132 --> 00:00:49,132
Whatever it draws becomes your desktop.

15
00:00:49,993 --> 00:00:52,513
And this isn't a trick
someone found last year.

16
00:00:53,233 --> 00:00:59,253
In 1990, Windows 3.0 had the same switch
in a plain text file called system.ini.

17
00:00:59,873 --> 00:01:01,273
One line: shell=progman.exe.

18
00:01:04,363 --> 00:01:06,883
If you changed that name, Program Manager,

19
00:01:07,123 --> 00:01:10,283
the window full of icon groups,
never appeared at all.

20
00:01:10,995 --> 00:01:14,235
So the question isn't whether Windows
can have a different desktop.

21
00:01:14,855 --> 00:01:16,055
It always could.

22
00:01:16,495 --> 00:01:19,575
The question is why almost
nobody has seen one.

23
00:01:21,885 --> 00:01:24,585
Start with what that line
actually points to.

24
00:01:25,305 --> 00:01:28,985
The taskbar, the desktop icons,
the little tray by the clock:

25
00:01:29,205 --> 00:01:31,625
all of that is one program, Explorer.

26
00:01:32,245 --> 00:01:35,745
Yes, the same Explorer
you open to look through your files.

27
00:01:36,205 --> 00:01:40,325
It does two jobs,
and most people only ever notice one.

28
00:01:40,876 --> 00:01:45,296
That second job has a name: the shell.
It's the layer you touch.

29
00:01:46,216 --> 00:01:47,896
Underneath it sit the kernel, the drivers,

30
00:01:48,126 --> 00:01:50,756
and the services
that keep Windows running.

31
00:01:51,387 --> 00:01:53,247
None of them need a taskbar.

32
00:01:53,407 --> 00:01:57,667
End Explorer in Task Manager,
and the taskbar and icons vanish...

33
00:01:58,007 --> 00:02:01,607
but Notepad, your browser,
every other window keeps working.

34
00:02:02,258 --> 00:02:04,918
Start it again,
and the desktop comes back.

35
00:02:05,418 --> 00:02:09,318
That's about the clearest proof there is
that the desktop is just another program.

36
00:02:12,168 --> 00:02:16,908
If you've ever changed the launcher on an
Android phone, you've seen a similar idea.

37
00:02:17,568 --> 00:02:19,788
Android makes it a normal setting.

38
00:02:20,468 --> 00:02:22,388
Windows has the mechanism too,

39
00:02:22,468 --> 00:02:25,708
but never gave ordinary users
a simple button for it.

40
00:02:26,641 --> 00:02:28,561
Three words get mixed up here.

41
00:02:29,141 --> 00:02:33,341
The shell is what you launch things from:
the taskbar, the menus, the desktop.

42
00:02:34,081 --> 00:02:38,741
A window manager controls how windows
are positioned, stacked and focused.

43
00:02:39,661 --> 00:02:42,901
A desktop environment
brings the whole experience together.

44
00:02:43,618 --> 00:02:47,438
On Linux, these pieces can be combined
in many different ways.

45
00:02:48,058 --> 00:02:51,498
On Windows, replacing the shell doesn't
replace the system's window management,

46
00:02:51,728 --> 00:02:55,138
or the way applications
draw their window frames.

47
00:02:57,798 --> 00:02:59,938
That didn't stop people from trying.

48
00:03:00,538 --> 00:03:03,078
By the early nineties,
you could walk into a shop

49
00:03:03,308 --> 00:03:05,858
and buy a replacement for Program Manager.

50
00:03:06,536 --> 00:03:09,656
Norton Desktop for Windows
gave you its own desktop,

51
00:03:10,076 --> 00:03:14,916
its own file tools and its own launcher,
on top of the same Windows underneath.

52
00:03:15,677 --> 00:03:19,857
Some computer makers replaced it
before the customer ever switched on.

53
00:03:20,597 --> 00:03:22,457
Packard Bell's Navigator greeted buyers

54
00:03:22,687 --> 00:03:25,737
with a house you could walk through,
room by room.

55
00:03:26,714 --> 00:03:29,754
Then Windows 95 handed
the job to Explorer,

56
00:03:29,914 --> 00:03:34,514
and the line in system.ini
simply changed to explorer.exe.

57
00:03:35,354 --> 00:03:37,774
The switch stayed exactly where it was.

58
00:03:40,054 --> 00:03:43,774
In the late nineties, a whole
community grew up around that switch.

59
00:03:44,574 --> 00:03:46,414
The best-known project was LiteStep.

60
00:03:47,576 --> 00:03:51,436
It borrowed its look from AfterStep,
a Unix window manager modelled

61
00:03:51,666 --> 00:03:56,156
on NeXTSTEP, from the company
Steve Jobs founded after leaving Apple.

62
00:03:56,845 --> 00:04:00,185
LiteStep had no Start
menu unless you built one.

63
00:04:00,545 --> 00:04:04,145
Everything was a module:
a dock here, a clock there,

64
00:04:04,225 --> 00:04:06,285
a menu that opened
wherever you right-clicked.

65
00:04:07,503 --> 00:04:09,803
Its themes were shared like wallpapers.

66
00:04:10,183 --> 00:04:11,863
Some looked futuristic.

67
00:04:12,523 --> 00:04:14,503
Some looked like a Unix workstation.

68
00:04:15,003 --> 00:04:17,963
Some left almost nothing
on the screen at all.

69
00:04:18,549 --> 00:04:21,029
Blackbox for Windows went the other way.

70
00:04:21,509 --> 00:04:24,869
It copied Blackbox,
a minimalist Unix window manager,

71
00:04:24,949 --> 00:04:27,909
down to a single plain menu
and a slim toolbar.

72
00:04:28,652 --> 00:04:33,772
Its successor, bbLean, kept that idea
alive on Windows 2000 and XP.

73
00:04:34,764 --> 00:04:38,684
Emerge Desktop broke the shell
into separate parts: a launcher,

74
00:04:38,764 --> 00:04:43,004
a tray, a task list, each one a small
program you could keep or drop.

75
00:04:44,207 --> 00:04:45,767
Why go to all that trouble?

76
00:04:46,247 --> 00:04:50,527
Partly because the look of XP
was easy to recognise and hard to change.

77
00:04:51,367 --> 00:04:53,587
Themes changed the colours and buttons.

78
00:04:54,327 --> 00:04:55,587
The layout stayed.

79
00:04:56,277 --> 00:04:57,497
Partly memory.

80
00:04:57,757 --> 00:05:00,777
On a machine with a hundred
and twenty-eight megabytes,

81
00:05:00,857 --> 00:05:03,897
a lighter shell promised more room
for everything else.

82
00:05:04,281 --> 00:05:08,541
And partly Linux.
There, choosing your desktop was normal,

83
00:05:08,621 --> 00:05:11,221
and some Windows users wanted
the same choice.

84
00:05:12,531 --> 00:05:14,531
Most of those projects are gone now.

85
00:05:14,761 --> 00:05:19,411
LiteStep's last release was a preview
from 2014, still marked alpha.

86
00:05:19,641 --> 00:05:23,894
Emerge Desktop
and SharpEnviro stopped too.

87
00:05:25,794 --> 00:05:28,554
Small volunteer teams
were part of the reason.

88
00:05:28,874 --> 00:05:31,034
But Windows changed too.

89
00:05:31,514 --> 00:05:35,454
Over the years, its desktop became
much more than one executable.

90
00:05:36,305 --> 00:05:40,185
The desktop experience is now spread
across multiple processes,

91
00:05:40,385 --> 00:05:43,165
system components
and packaged applications.

92
00:05:44,225 --> 00:05:47,405
Even the Start menu runs
as a program of its own.

93
00:05:47,944 --> 00:05:49,824
Replacing Explorer doesn't mean replacing

94
00:05:50,054 --> 00:05:53,204
everything that makes up
the modern Windows interface.

95
00:05:54,024 --> 00:05:56,944
And that's one reason why building
a complete alternative desktop

96
00:05:57,174 --> 00:05:59,444
is harder than it used to be.

97
00:06:01,994 --> 00:06:03,674
And yet it still works.

98
00:06:04,314 --> 00:06:08,654
Cairo Desktop, an open-source project,
runs on Windows 11 today.

99
00:06:09,324 --> 00:06:12,784
It brings its own menu bar along the top,
its own taskbar,

100
00:06:12,864 --> 00:06:15,824
and folders you can pin
to the bar as stacks.

101
00:06:16,631 --> 00:06:20,111
It can run alongside Explorer,
or take over as the shell.

102
00:06:21,211 --> 00:06:24,231
In that second mode,
parts of Windows 11 can misbehave,

103
00:06:24,461 --> 00:06:27,471
Settings and Windows Security among them.

104
00:06:28,593 --> 00:06:30,793
Seelen UI goes about it differently.

105
00:06:31,653 --> 00:06:33,373
It doesn't replace Explorer.

106
00:06:34,273 --> 00:06:36,833
It hides the taskbar
and brings its own pieces:

107
00:06:37,033 --> 00:06:41,133
a dock, a bar, widgets, and a window
manager that tiles your windows.

108
00:06:41,877 --> 00:06:44,037
The result can look nothing like Windows.

109
00:06:44,597 --> 00:06:47,177
But underneath, Explorer is still running.

110
00:06:47,897 --> 00:06:49,897
It's a new face, not a new shell.

111
00:06:52,417 --> 00:06:53,617
And Microsoft?

112
00:06:54,177 --> 00:06:56,597
It actually has an official
feature for this.

113
00:06:57,297 --> 00:07:01,517
It's called Shell Launcher,
and it's designed for devices like kiosks.

114
00:07:02,053 --> 00:07:04,713
You've probably used something
like it without knowing.

115
00:07:05,113 --> 00:07:10,853
The ticket machine at a station.
A self-service checkout. A cash machine.

116
00:07:11,477 --> 00:07:15,577
Many of them are Windows PCs
whose whole shell is one application.

117
00:07:16,657 --> 00:07:18,457
An administrator names the program,

118
00:07:18,537 --> 00:07:21,897
and after sign-in,
that program is the whole desktop.

119
00:07:22,553 --> 00:07:27,913
But it's only available in the Enterprise,
Education and IoT Enterprise editions.

120
00:07:28,551 --> 00:07:31,951
And the older registry mechanism
is still part of Windows.

121
00:07:32,791 --> 00:07:37,491
Microsoft just never turned it into a
normal desktop setting for ordinary users.

122
00:07:40,791 --> 00:07:43,851
So why does Windows only
seem to have one desktop?

123
00:07:44,411 --> 00:07:46,771
Not because it can't have another.

124
00:07:47,191 --> 00:07:49,171
Because the desktop is the product.

125
00:07:52,201 --> 00:07:54,461
On Linux, the desktop is a choice.

126
00:07:55,241 --> 00:07:58,661
Some distributions ask you to pick one
while you're still installing.

127
00:07:59,361 --> 00:08:02,461
KDE Plasma, GNOME, Xfce, Cinnamon...

128
00:08:03,138 --> 00:08:05,618
On Windows,
the desktop is what Microsoft shows

129
00:08:05,848 --> 00:08:10,098
in every advert, every screenshot
and every support page.

130
00:08:11,038 --> 00:08:15,018
One desktop means one set of instructions
for more than a billion machines.

131
00:08:15,908 --> 00:08:17,488
That's a real advantage.

132
00:08:18,308 --> 00:08:21,888
When something breaks, the screen
in the help article looks like yours.

133
00:08:22,548 --> 00:08:25,708
The cost is that most people never learn
there was a choice.

134
00:08:28,338 --> 00:08:32,958
People were swapping the Windows desktop
before Windows 95 even existed.

135
00:08:33,818 --> 00:08:38,018
They built docks, bare menus, and desktops
that looked like nothing else.

136
00:08:38,612 --> 00:08:42,432
Most of those projects are gone.
The idea isn't.

137
00:08:42,592 --> 00:08:44,912
Under every taskbar you've ever clicked,

138
00:08:44,992 --> 00:08:47,972
there's still that one setting,
waiting for a different name.

139
00:08:49,168 --> 00:08:51,728
The desktop is just
another piece of software.

140
00:08:51,808 --> 00:08:54,148
And software can be replaced.

141
00:08:55,878 --> 00:08:57,678
So here's a question for you.

142
00:08:58,358 --> 00:09:01,378
If Windows let you pick your desktop
while installing, the way some

143
00:09:01,608 --> 00:09:05,158
Linux distributions do, would you switch,
or keep the one you know?

144
00:09:05,458 --> 00:09:07,008
Let me know in the comments.
